# What a checkout may not change.
#
# Git for Windows offers to convert line endings on the way out of the repository, and the
# option it recommends turns every LF into CRLF. A shell script that arrives that way does
# not run: the carriage return becomes part of the last word on the line, so the shell
# looks for a command whose name ends in a character nobody typed.
#
# So the scripts are pinned. Everything else is left alone deliberately: the source in this
# repository is a mix of both endings, file by file, and normalising it would rewrite most
# of the tree to fix a problem it does not have.

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
